You are the **auditor** of round $CF_AUDIT_ROUND (of at most $CF_AUDIT_ROUNDS) for task `tasks/$TEND_TASK_ID.yaml`, in worktree $TEND_WORKTREE_PATH. Judge the cut of `$CF_AUDIT_DOCUMENT` against the spec in your system prompt, one formal passage at a time.

## The data
`.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.render.part<i>.md` renders the document against its layer, in parts; "This call" below names your part and the passages it holds. Every passage appears in span order:

- `[n] item · span · scope · kind · prominence · section`;
- `TEXT`, the span exactly (a line break shown as `⏎`), then `STATEMENT`, and on a compound statement `SHAPE` with its conjuncts;
- `HINT`: `kind-other` (a formal passage of kind `other`), `kind-rule` (one in a proof or example section, which the merge gives a `wrong_kind` verdict unless you say `other` is right), `in-proof`, `in-example`, `connective-start` (it starts at a connective), `widened` (it runs past its block only by the gate's widest bound);
- before it, a `GAP` line: layer text no passage holds.

Read your whole part, in pieces with offset and limit when it is long.

## What you write
The file "This call" names, `.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.audit.part<i>.json`:

```json
{"schema": "{{audit_schema}}", "kind": "audit", "round": $CF_AUDIT_ROUND, "source_id": "$CF_AUDIT_SOURCE",
 "passages": [
   {"n": 12, "item": "In-text #0000c0de", "origin": "statement_section", "verdict": "over_cut",
    "rule": 1, "merge_with": "In-text #3fa91c07",
    "reason": "one statement with the definition after it",
    "action": "merge into #3fa91c07; keep this start; end after 'spanning gadget.'"},
   {"n": 13, "item": "In-text #3fa91c07", "origin": "statement_section", "verdict": "clean",
    "rule": 0, "merge_with": "", "reason": "", "action": ""}],
 "expository_flags": [],
 "per_spec_formal_estimate": 41, "patterns": [],
 "subject_check": "one subject passage, the first that states the theorem"}
```

1. **`passages`: one row for EVERY `formal_math` passage of your part**, and for no other passage, with:
   - `origin`, where it sits: one of {{audit_origins}};
   - `verdict`: `clean` when the spec permits this passage as cut, scoped, kinded and stated; `over_cut` when it and a neighbour are one statement (C1), or its cut breaks a C4 NEVER, a C3 chain or a C5 compound list (on the passage that merges INTO the neighbour `merge_with` names, once per merge); `under_cut` when it holds two results; `should_be_expository` when it is narration, set-up, a lead-in, history or a description rather than a statement (C6, C7, C9); `should_be_non_math`; `redundant` when it only restates what a neighbouring passage states (C10, or a duplicate); `wrong_kind`; `wrong_prominence`; `statement_misstates` when its `statement` says something the text does not, or leaves out a hypothesis or object it needs to read on its own (C7);
   - `rule`, the number of the rule the verdict rests on (0 for `clean`);
   - `reason`, at most {{reason_words}} words, and `action`, at most {{action_words}} words a worker can apply without judging again ("merge into #3fa91c07; keep this start; end after '…'", "split at 'Moreover' into a theorem and a notation", "set passage_kind theorem", "scope expository"); both empty on a clean row.
2. **The kind rule.** Judge every passage marked `HINT kind-other` under C6 explicitly. When `other` is right for it, write `"kind_other_ok": true` on its row (`clean`, unless another verdict applies) and say why in `reason`; otherwise it is `wrong_kind`, rule {{kind_rule_rule}}. A `kind-rule` passage you leave with neither gets a `wrong_kind` verdict from the merge.
3. **`widened`.** A passage marked `HINT widened` crosses its block only because the gate's widest bound lets it: judge whether its sentence really runs on into the block it crosses (C2), and flag it when not.
4. **`expository_flags`**: every passage of your part scoped `expository` or `non_math` that the spec makes `formal_math`, as `{"item", "rule", "reason", "action"}`.
5. **`per_spec_formal_estimate`**: how many `formal_math` passages a cut that follows the spec exactly would have in your part; **`patterns`**: at most {{max_patterns}} systematic tendencies you saw, each with an example item; **`subject_check`**: whether the subject marker is on at most one passage, the first that states the theorem or definition (C9), as far as your part shows.

Then run the validator line below until it passes, and stop: no result file, no commit.
